1. Keep functions small and focused. Avoid functions longer than 30 lines unless strictly necessary.
2. Use clear, descriptive variable and method names. Avoid generic names like 'data', 'temp', 'val'.
3. Avoid deeply nested blocks. Use early returns and helper methods instead of nested if-else or try-catch blocks beyond 2 levels.
4. Do not modify or refactor any existing methods, classes, or logic unless explicitly asked to. Preserve current behavior at all times.
5. Do not delete or rename any existing files, classes, or methods.
6. Only use classes, functions, and imports that already exist in the project or are from standard/approved libraries. Do not hallucinate new APIs.
7. All imports must correspond to real, existing libraries or modules. No fabricated packages or symbols.
8. Break complex logic into reusable helper methods or services. Keep responsibilities clearly separated.
9. Every new method, class, or module must be accompanied by corresponding unit or integration tests.
10. Follow consistent coding style (indentation, spacing, naming) based on the existing codebase.
11. Comments should be used only where the code is not self-explanatory. Avoid redundant comments.
12. Avoid clever or overly abstract solutions. Prefer readable and maintainable code over smart shortcuts.
 